<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="38246" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/PS/377/38246.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2025" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025.xml">2025</calendarYear><term id="120251" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring.xml">Spring 2025</term><subject id="PS" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/PS.xml">Political Science</subject><course id="377" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/PS/377.xml">Topics Contemp Pol Theory</course></parents><sectionNumber>A  </sectionNumber><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Major restrictions will be lifted at 12pm on Thursday, November 14, 2024. Fascisms and anti-fascisms. Talk of fascism and anti-fascism has acquired new currency in our present moment of polycrisis. But what do people mean when they warn of fascism? And what are debates about whether fascism can happen here, in the United States, really about? This course explores historical and recent scholarship on fascism and anti-fascism to shed light on these issues.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>Closed</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2025-01-21Z</startDate><endDate>2025-05-07Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>11:00 AM</start><end>12:20 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>TR     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>336</roomNumber><buildingName>Davenport Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Erlenbusch-Anderson" firstName="V">Erlenbusch-Anderson, V</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>